---
import Base from '../layouts/base.astro';

const cssCode = `.my-editor-wrapper {
  --beaket-paper-accent: #0c6bae;     /* links, focus, selection tint */
  --beaket-paper-ink: #232a35;        /* body text + caret */
  --beaket-paper-paper: #ffffff;      /* rendered surface */
  --beaket-paper-font: Georgia, serif;
  --beaket-paper-font-size: 18px;
  --beaket-paper-measure: 42rem;      /* max line width; default none = full width */
}`;
---

<Base title="Styling — Paper">
  <h1>Styling</h1>

  <p>
    The editor ships its own visual tokens via a CodeMirror theme scoped to <code>.cm-editor</code>, so
    it works standalone — no CSS file to import.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tokens">CSS variables</h2>
  <p>
    Every token resolves through <code>var(--beaket-paper-X, …)</code>. Set any of them on
    <code>:root</code> or any ancestor of the editor and it follows — no <code>!important</code>, no
    specificity fights:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{cssCode}</code></pre>
  <blockquote>
    <code>letter-spacing</code> is intentionally <strong>not</strong> exposed — negative spacing breaks
    mixed-script CJK, so it is fixed at <code>0</code> (a CJK-first guard).
  </blockquote>

  <h2 id="bridge">Design-token bridge</h2>
  <p>
    If the host app already exposes a <code>--color-*</code> design-token palette, the editor bridges to
    it automatically — including dark mode — with no setup, so it inherits your theme. Each token
    resolves three tiers deep: <code>var(--beaket-paper-X, var(--color-Y, default))</code> — your
    explicit override first, then the host palette, then a built-in default.
  </p>

  <h2 id="escape">Escape hatches</h2>
  <p>For anything the variables don't cover:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Style the stable <code>.cm-*</code> class hooks directly — e.g. <code>.cm-table-widget</code>, <code>.cm-slash-menu</code>, <code>.cm-md-copy</code>.</li>
    <li>Reach the underlying CodeMirror <code>EditorView</code> via <code>ref.current.getView()</code> to add your own extensions or theme.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
